+## 7. The handoff shape (one for every handoff, 2026-10-17)
+
+Every handoff, whatever carries it (a mailbox message, a batch item, a board item asked for in a final report, a review request), states these six fields. They mostly rename fields that already exist; nothing new is filed. Proposed in company/decisions/2026-10-17-onboarding-reconciliation.md (founder ratifies the working-agreement text that points here).
+
+| Field | What it holds | Where it already lives |
+|---|---|---|
+| run | the task or run id that produced the handoff (agent label, `mail:<id>`, `batch=<file>#B<n>`, or W-id) | event summary, mailbox file name |
+| input | the artifact handed over, with its revision: path plus commit SHA or lesson version (`v3`) | batch `inputs`; mailbox body |
+| output | what is asked for, in one line (a record, a verdict, a build) | batch item title; mailbox body |
+| next owner | the one role that accepts or rejects it (not "the team") | `to:` / batch `role` / board `--owner` |
+| accept when | the check that makes it done, readable without the sender (a command, a verdict word, a file) | `done-when:` |
+| due | a date or a trigger ("before PR2", "next round"); none means it is not a commitment | new; write `due: none` if there is none |
+
+The next owner answers **accepted**, **rejected (why)** or **done (evidence)** in its own record or report; silence is not acceptance. Evaluator handoffs carry the artifact only (`input`), never the builder's account (working agreement item 3). Delivery is not acceptance: count a handoff as wired only when its acceptance is on disk.
